WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.713
- Hi everyone, good afternoon.

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:11.870
I am utterly delighted to invite you all

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:15.390
to the follow-up discussion
session and conversation

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.230
from yesterday's excellent presentation

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:20.420
by Dr. Sarah Teichmann.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.300
On inference, the nature of inference,

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.010
and rather, The Inference of Nature

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.740
Cause and Effect in Molecular Biology.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:33.910
And we are delighted to
have as a conversance today,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.870
professor Neil Lawrence, who
is the DeepMind professor

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.793
of Machine Learning at the
University of Cambridge.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:44.020
Professor Lawrence is the
inaugural DeepMind professor

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:45.550
at the University of Cambridge,

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.000
He has been working on
machine learning models

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.020
for over 20 years.

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.100
He recently returned to
Academia after three years

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.270
as the director of
machine learning at Amazon

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:58.750
and his main interest has
been in the interaction

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.630
of machine learning
with the physical world.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.570
And this interest of his was triggered by

00:01:04.570 --> 00:01:08.300
the deploying of machine
learning in the African context,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.570
which I'm quite curious to
hear more about from him

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.530
when end to end solutions
are normally required

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.130
and he has inspired his work,

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.800
has inspired many new
directions, research directions

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.670
at the interface of machine
learning and systems research

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.280
and a lot of his work is funded

00:01:31.136 --> 00:01:35.440
with an AI fellowship from
the Alan Turing Institute.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:39.790
He is also very well
vetted, has many awards

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:42.210
and recognition.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:45.260
And I won't dwell on
those because I'm dying

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:50.260
to get into conversation and
participate in the discussion

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.083
that he and professor
Teichmann are going to have.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:59.770
So Neil, Sarah, absolutely delighted

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:02.040
to have you both here this afternoon

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.040
and I'm looking forward to a
smooth intellectually choppy,

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:13.260
but a hopefully a smooth
presentation this evening.

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.203
Thank you both.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:19.050
- Thanks so much Priya and
thanks very much for the

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.790
invite to be the conversant and for Sarah

00:02:21.790 --> 00:02:24.323
also for great presentation yesterday.

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.480
So I'm gonna open with a
sort of a short discussion.

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:31.400
I won't use slides,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:35.620
but I wanted to riff off the
opening to one of the questions

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:40.620
which was William Kwok said
before he phrased this question,

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:44.670
he said, I'm not a real
scientist, I'm a social scientist.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.693
And I kind of think that that's,

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.883
there's a problem at the heart
of what he's saying there.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.160
Because we're in a world where

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:55.710
there's many, many complex things

00:02:55.710 --> 00:02:58.570
we'd like to understand
and make decisions on.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.960
And yet some of them apparently
fall beyond the remit

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:05.310
of what science is capable of
answering because effectively

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:07.270
it's not William, that's letting us down,

00:03:07.270 --> 00:03:09.100
it's science, that's letting William down

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.680
if he feels that to be the case.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.330
And I think what's so exciting
about Sarah's presentation

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:16.350
yesterday and what we're seeing

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.230
in the sort of the shift
in biology to a much more,

00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:22.080
I mean, it's always had
quantitative elements,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.210
but the dominance of quantitative work

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.600
in terms of scientific discovery,

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.250
I think it has to be our hope

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.070
that the same type of shift will occur

00:03:32.070 --> 00:03:33.660
with a sort of move towards computational

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.360
and quantitative social science.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.020
And that's not, by the way,
as I hope I'll highlight,

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:40.810
as I discussed to sort of
diminish qualitative research,

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.660
I think that's absolutely vital.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:45.720
But I thought that was a sort
of interesting starting point

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.860
because what's really going on

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:50.200
and what we saw a lot with
what Sarah talked about

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.880
is the complexity of systems

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.950
we're trying to be scientific about.

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.860
And if you think Sarah sort of ended

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:57.360
talking about immune system,

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.900
I mean, it's one of my
favorite refs to sort of say,

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.540
what do you think we now
understand the brain?

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:03.610
You should hear what we don't
know about the immune system.

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:05.400
And the immune system is

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.940
sort of widespread across
the all animal kingdoms

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:10.760
and anticipated extraordinary
type of intelligence,

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:12.560
but not just the complexity
of those systems,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:13.590
but also the answer of,

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:15.920
well, if we're talking about
inference about the systems,

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.640
what is knowledge itself?

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.400
So what does it mean to
understand those things?

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:23.130
And I think that that
also came through a lot

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:25.610
in what Sarah was saying.

00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:27.920
And I think particularly
you sit in biological work.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:29.930
So part of what that means is perhaps

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:32.340
to be able to predict what might happen

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.530
in the case of an
intervention in that system.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.740
And you see that a lot
in biological models,

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:40.880
but Sarah actually used a
number of different ways

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.380
of trying to explain how
these systems operate.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.370
And the first couple she used
was what I would talk about

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:48.830
is dimensionality reduction

00:04:49.733 --> 00:04:51.030
and giving you a map
as she would call that.

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.440
And it's so interesting

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.890
and it goes all the way back to Gauss

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:54.930
with mapping technologies

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:57.520
and Gauss was actually doing
it for mapping the earth.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.570
But what we're often trying to do

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:01.230
is build a map, which means

00:05:01.230 --> 00:05:02.830
that the sort of functional approximation

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:04.540
from a low dimensional space

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.260
to a high dimensional space in
which we collected the data.

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:09.240
And it's interesting
the extent we use that

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:11.190
analogy almost unthinkingly
very, very often.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:12.810
I don't think Sarah was thinking about

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.817
this sort of cartographic
map when she said that

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.870
but of course, one of
the fantastic projects

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:21.840
that she's been leading
is the human Silas.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:23.410
So there's outlets again,

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:25.280
we see these analogies coming forward

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.770
and it's because there
is a genuine relationship

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:31.970
between that practice that
we're mostly all familiar with,

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.300
or maybe with Google maps,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.170
we've all forgotten how to read a map,

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.320
but it's the practice that
we're mostly familiar with

00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:38.827
of an abstraction of the world.

00:05:38.827 --> 00:05:40.553
One of my favorite, I can't remember

00:05:40.553 --> 00:05:41.920
where this story comes
from in the first place,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.577
but by the (indistinct) with
the one that I heard it from,

00:05:45.577 --> 00:05:47.377
and it's not from him originally was

00:05:48.390 --> 00:05:51.300
the notion of the country
that was so good at maps.

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:53.110
He said, when you build them a 150,000,

00:05:53.110 --> 00:05:55.140
well, we started going with one to 10,000

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:55.973
that wasn't good enough

00:05:55.973 --> 00:05:58.400
and then we decided one to
5,000 that wasn't good enough.

00:05:58.400 --> 00:06:00.370
One to 1000, that wasn't good enough.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:03.140
Eventually we've gone
for a one to one map.

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:03.980
(laughs)

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.570
- I think that's Bo Herse's story.

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:07.403
Yeah.

00:06:07.403 --> 00:06:08.346
- That's brilliant.

00:06:08.346 --> 00:06:09.179
Thank you Priya, for comment.

00:06:09.179 --> 00:06:10.890
I was forgetting, I would end up there.

00:06:10.890 --> 00:06:12.570
So that's great that we've got the erupts.

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:15.100
And of course that's
nonsensical, that's useless.

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.011
The map is an obstruction

00:06:17.011 --> 00:06:19.140
and then abstraction that
is used for some purpose.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:20.690
So this knowledge that we're interested in

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.410
is therefore somehow contextual

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.950
for what we choose to abstract

00:06:24.950 --> 00:06:26.490
is depend on who we are

00:06:26.490 --> 00:06:28.790
and what questions we're
interested in asking.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:33.740
Sarah very brilliantly went
on to sort of talk about,

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.930
I thought one of the most
remarkable pieces work

00:06:35.930 --> 00:06:38.411
from my point of view was the work on

00:06:38.411 --> 00:06:42.320
the way the dimers and
the timers come together.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:43.960
And this sort of leading
to this predictive

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.470
almost like periodic table
of the protein structures.

00:06:48.470 --> 00:06:50.040
And I think it's sort of interesting,

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:53.000
again, we see the need
to convert our knowledge

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:55.000
into some sort of system
that we can relate to

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:55.937
the original periodic table.

00:06:55.937 --> 00:06:57.890
And of course, and she talked about Mendel

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:01.100
and Daley and genetics
and Mendelian inheritance,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.930
which is actually quite a nice,
simple form of inheritance,

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.470
but it's actually quite distant
from the form of inheritance

00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.653
that we see in much of animal life.

00:07:10.528 --> 00:07:12.440
And I think there's an
interesting aspect there

00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:15.930
that we are drawn to
obviously explanations

00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:17.350
that we can understand.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:21.780
The bookshelf, the map,
the Mendelian genetics,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.020
the periodic table.

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.920
And I think there's a sort
of follow up question,

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:28.550
but is that really the way the world is?

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:31.760
Or is it just the windows that we can see

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:33.860
through when we look at the world,

00:07:33.860 --> 00:07:36.370
the things that enable
us to have intuition

00:07:36.370 --> 00:07:38.370
about what's happening in the world.

00:07:38.370 --> 00:07:40.480
And I don't know the
answer to that question

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.070
and perhaps the answer
doesn't really matter

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:45.700
'cause it's sort of more
around for amorphic comment,

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:47.110
but it perhaps does matter very much

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:49.175
in the practice of science
when we're practically,

00:07:49.175 --> 00:07:50.890
as Priya said in the introduction,

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:53.060
I'm very keen on what happens

00:07:53.060 --> 00:07:54.840
when real-world decision-making comes in

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:57.160
and Sarah again, showed
a wonderful example

00:07:57.160 --> 00:07:59.040
of how you can use these technologies

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:02.947
in perhaps diagnosing
COVID and other diseases

00:08:02.947 --> 00:08:06.130
and in future other diseases
of the immune system.

00:08:06.130 --> 00:08:08.880
If we can use understanding
of the human cell Atlas

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.770
to understand how we're being infected,

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:12.470
well, how wonderful would that be?

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.540
But this leads me on to
sort of why I think two

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.810
really emerging challenges
are in the domain here,

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:20.730
which I think Sarah was
massively dealing with.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.000
And she's the head of her field,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.400
but these are just really challenging

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.150
and they keep coming up everywhere.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:29.060
And there's two paradoxes

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:33.800
that I talk about as the big data paradox.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:35.730
And then I have a follow on paradox

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.380
called a big model paradox.

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:38.960
So Sarah very nicely described

00:08:38.960 --> 00:08:41.680
how the data and model interact.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.540
And I tend to think of this as a spiral.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.960
And there's a wonderful
quote from Carl Popper,

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:47.770
which is the answer to the question.

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.240
I can't remember whether
he uses hypothesis.

00:08:50.240 --> 00:08:51.390
I think he used hypothesis,

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:52.830
which comes first to the hypothesis

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:56.720
or the experiment to which I
would say the model or the data

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.500
is the same as the answer to
the question which comes first,

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:05.290
the chicken or the egg, the
answer is that they co-evolve.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:07.570
So what do we mean by that is early on,

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:10.120
there is a very diffused
kind of experiment,

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.700
which unfortunately, some
people call fishing expeditions.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:16.010
Well, it's a diffuse experiment
that's trying to understand

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:16.843
where should one focus

00:09:16.843 --> 00:09:19.750
and that I really dislike
that term fishing expedition.

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.960
And then once you've done
that initial experiment

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:23.800
that might inform you,

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:25.190
which is informed by some understanding

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.680
the system that helps focus,

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:28.510
that helps refine your
understanding of the system

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:29.343
and you focus down and down.

00:09:29.343 --> 00:09:33.150
And I see this as a spiral moving forward,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:34.360
but what we're trying...

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:36.470
One of the challenges we're experiencing

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:39.460
is we get more and more data in the world,

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:41.390
it strikes me that we seem to understand

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:42.610
less and less about the world

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:44.690
and there are many examples about this.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.840
So for example, we could think
about election predictions.

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:50.250
You'll be getting more
and more information

00:09:50.250 --> 00:09:51.620
about who's clicking on what,

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:53.270
what people are saying on Twitter

00:09:53.270 --> 00:09:55.273
and yet we seem to be,

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.470
if anything, getting worse

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:58.990
at predicting the outcome of elections.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:01.880
And this is quite a well
understood statistical concept,

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:03.660
how you predict the outcome of elections,

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:04.860
so that's kind of odd.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:06.890
Or stratified medicine.

00:10:06.890 --> 00:10:10.930
So trying to sort of target
drugs at the right population,

00:10:10.930 --> 00:10:12.810
as we get more and more
information about patients,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.570
you'd hope that we could get
better and better at that,

00:10:14.570 --> 00:10:16.500
and that doesn't seem to be happening.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:18.020
And I think the reason is the following.

00:10:18.020 --> 00:10:21.200
So I observed this some years
ago and spoke about it first

00:10:22.213 --> 00:10:23.920
at a talk of data science
Africa as Priya said.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.620
A lot of my interests are inspired

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:27.570
by applications in the African context.

00:10:28.520 --> 00:10:30.310
But I couldn't sort of give an answer to,

00:10:30.310 --> 00:10:31.207
is what I think is going on.

00:10:31.207 --> 00:10:34.106
And it was actually
during the COVID pandemic

00:10:34.106 --> 00:10:37.470
that I've come up with
this, my hesitant answer.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:39.220
And it comes from realization

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:41.240
the same thing happens with modals.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:43.430
So the big data paradox, I think,

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.610
is arising from the follow-up.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:48.540
It's the sort of example of not being able

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.210
to see the wood for the trees or tree.

00:10:51.210 --> 00:10:52.530
So what happens is we gain

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:55.060
more and more data about a phenomenal,

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.630
and therefore we get
more and more interested

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.600
in this data we have and
perhaps more and more focused.

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:02.040
This is sort of data in
attention, blind, that's going on.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:04.690
We've become very focused
on that data we've required

00:11:05.599 --> 00:11:07.940
and we think we've got
a very rich description,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.830
but the description is not representative

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.690
of the wider system.

00:11:11.690 --> 00:11:13.470
And as a result, we ended up making

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:14.650
very, very biased decisions.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.270
We talked a little bit about bias in data,

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:17.290
and one of the ways that,

00:11:17.290 --> 00:11:19.070
and Sarah does a brilliant job

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:21.270
bringing these data sets
together, which is absolutely key,

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:23.910
but not many people
are doing that as well.

00:11:23.910 --> 00:11:25.870
You get this very sort of focused dataset

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.610
on one small aspect of the entity.

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:29.430
You believe you've understood a great deal

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:31.550
because you've got so much
data, but in practice,

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:34.670
you've actually just understood
a lot about the wrong thing.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:36.450
So you perhaps you wanted to understand

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.300
the small ecosystem
around a tree in a forest,

00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.950
but what you actually
ended up understanding

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:43.640
is some of the details of
how caterpillar is operating

00:11:43.640 --> 00:11:44.473
on one of its leaves,

00:11:44.473 --> 00:11:47.390
which is not relevant to
the question of interest.

00:11:47.390 --> 00:11:48.810
So you can see how that relates

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:51.540
to this notion of abstraction and the map.

00:11:51.540 --> 00:11:53.260
The danger is you go diving in

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:54.470
and what's happening is people are losing

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:57.960
their human intuition about
what goes on in these systems.

00:11:57.960 --> 00:11:59.100
They go diving in and collect all the data

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.360
in the world without that intuition

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:02.710
and they collect the wrong type of data

00:12:02.710 --> 00:12:04.680
because they perform the
wrong type of obstruction.

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.630
They get them at one-to-one
scale in the wrong place.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.940
And very similar things
are happening with modeling

00:12:09.940 --> 00:12:11.320
and I believe we saw that a lot

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:14.030
in a pandemic modeling
and epidemic modeling,

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:16.840
where you have people
creating richer and richer

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.240
descriptions of how diseases

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:20.770
can move through the population.

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.020
But if those descriptions themselves

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:24.700
are all based on the idea that the disease

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.220
is moving through tart,
rather than aerosol,

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:31.530
most of the aspects of that
description is useless to you.

00:12:31.530 --> 00:12:34.710
And actually what you
want is in both cases,

00:12:34.710 --> 00:12:37.473
what you want is a model
that's more abstract

00:12:37.473 --> 00:12:39.700
that is taking account of less detail,

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:41.650
but is more understandable by everyone

00:12:41.650 --> 00:12:42.597
that needs to operate on that model.

00:12:42.597 --> 00:12:44.860
And that's not just the
designers of the model

00:12:44.860 --> 00:12:47.570
or the collectors of the
data, but the domain experts.

00:12:47.570 --> 00:12:49.250
So in COVID, that would be
public health officials.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.060
If you're saying to the
public health people,

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:52.710
you can't understand my model,

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:53.900
then you've got the wrong model.

00:12:53.900 --> 00:12:55.640
You need to be able to communicate to them

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:57.950
because they need to be
able to make decisions true.

00:12:57.950 --> 00:12:59.840
And I think that that's
something that goes beyond

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.900
just the, sort of the
need for us individually

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:03.740
to understand what's going on science,

00:13:03.740 --> 00:13:05.850
but it must also be an ability to explain

00:13:05.850 --> 00:13:08.770
to other scientists or
the general population

00:13:08.770 --> 00:13:11.020
about why these decisions are going on.

00:13:11.020 --> 00:13:13.460
So it's not just about
individual human understanding,

00:13:13.460 --> 00:13:15.527
it's about societal
understanding of what's going on

00:13:15.527 --> 00:13:19.110
and I think that's gonna
become increasingly important.

