01:20:27.060 --> 01:20:29.000
it's by number and stale of forests,

01:20:29.000 --> 01:20:30.360
perhaps less the individual trees.

01:20:30.360 --> 01:20:32.240
It depends what I'm doing.

01:20:32.240 --> 01:20:34.600
And this is kind of what I
mean about meta reductionism.

01:20:34.600 --> 01:20:37.150
That it's subjective the answer to that,

01:20:37.150 --> 01:20:39.210
the problem we face with
the big data paradox.

01:20:39.210 --> 01:20:41.990
And it is something that
technically I'm interested

01:20:41.990 --> 01:20:43.540
in how you solve it and
actually got some prisons,

01:20:43.540 --> 01:20:45.280
latent variable loans to offer a solution.

01:20:45.280 --> 01:20:49.570
But this was a thing that
Sarah is using in her team

01:20:49.570 --> 01:20:52.240
is that you miss this ability to control

01:20:52.240 --> 01:20:53.650
whether you're looking
at the forest or the tree

01:20:53.650 --> 01:20:56.350
you like classical statistics
gives us the forest.

01:20:56.350 --> 01:20:59.680
We understand very well how it
gives us the forest and why.

01:20:59.680 --> 01:21:01.530
And then we think we're gonna get

01:21:01.530 --> 01:21:02.920
a little section of the forest,

01:21:02.920 --> 01:21:05.680
but we go immediately all
the way down to a leaf

01:21:05.680 --> 01:21:07.330
and some insect on it.

01:21:07.330 --> 01:21:10.140
We don't manage to get to
the sort of the structure

01:21:10.140 --> 01:21:13.140
we're interested in because
we don't have the sort of dial

01:21:13.140 --> 01:21:14.470
to move around these things.

01:21:14.470 --> 01:21:16.840
So I don't think it's
incoherent in a statement,

01:21:16.840 --> 01:21:19.750
but I do think what
you want is the ability

01:21:19.750 --> 01:21:21.950
to move smoothly across the spectrum.

01:21:21.950 --> 01:21:24.010
And you kind of want
that across all sciences

01:21:24.010 --> 01:21:26.540
and it relates very closely to this.

01:21:26.540 --> 01:21:28.750
I think that those spectra is
kind of like between physics

01:21:28.750 --> 01:21:32.000
and biology and things like that.

01:21:32.000 --> 01:21:35.133
- And I think we, let's close with that.

01:21:36.310 --> 01:21:38.830
Very, very nice question.

01:21:38.830 --> 01:21:39.663
And I think,

01:21:40.633 --> 01:21:43.700
I'm just very tempted to make
one small comment is that,

01:21:43.700 --> 01:21:46.150
often they have been sort of,

01:21:46.150 --> 01:21:50.090
the sociologists of science
have often critiqued

01:21:50.090 --> 01:21:53.650
narratives of science
that scientists offer

01:21:53.650 --> 01:21:56.270
in which they separate out the context.

01:21:56.270 --> 01:21:58.180
And the context doesn't really matter

01:21:58.180 --> 01:22:00.103
because of this sort of,

01:22:01.060 --> 01:22:05.230
slim notion and
understanding of objectivity.

01:22:05.230 --> 01:22:07.770
And I think that the
context really matters.

01:22:07.770 --> 01:22:10.440
And I think both Neil
and Sarah are telling us

01:22:11.451 --> 01:22:12.850
that the kinds of questions you ask

01:22:12.850 --> 01:22:14.230
and what questions you ask

01:22:14.230 --> 01:22:15.690
really depends on the context

01:22:15.690 --> 01:22:19.320
and what is it that
informs the question is

01:22:19.320 --> 01:22:23.757
almost as important as the
question itself, right?

01:22:23.757 --> 01:22:27.770
So thanks everyone for
this wonderful session.

01:22:27.770 --> 01:22:31.520
And I thank you both and see you all

01:22:31.520 --> 01:22:36.140
at the next next month colloquium, bye.

01:22:36.140 --> 01:22:37.143
Take care and-

01:22:38.148 --> 01:22:38.981
- Bye Priya.

01:22:38.981 --> 01:22:39.814
Thank you Neil.

